<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	App Sandbox is off deliberately, not omitted by oversight: RFD-19's
	architecture requires spawning and owning a long-lived Python worker
	subprocess (machinewright-worker) that lives outside the app bundle,
	in whatever per-project virtualenv the user's machine resolves. macOS
	App Sandbox disallows exec'ing arbitrary unbundled/unsigned executables
	regardless of file-access entitlements, which would break this
	fundamentally. Sandboxing is a Mac App Store distribution requirement,
	not a general macOS one — Machinewright, like other developer tools
	that shell out to project-local toolchains, is expected to distribute
	outside the App Store (Developer ID + notarization), where sandboxing
	is optional. Revisit only if App Store distribution is later required.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<false/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-only</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
